<article class="env-lemma" id="0CAB">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CAB">Lemma <span data-tag="0CAB">55.13.1</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/0C61" data-tag="0C61">55.9.3</a> let $d = \gcd (m_1, \ldots , m_ n)$. If $\mathcal{L}$ is an invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module which </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
restricts to the trivial invertible module on $C$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
has degree $0$ on each $C_ i$, 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> then $\mathcal{L}^{\otimes d} \cong \mathcal{O}_ X$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemma <a href="/tag/0C63" data-tag="0C63">55.9.5</a> we have $\mathcal{L} \cong \mathcal{O}_ X(\sum a_ i C_ i)$ for some $a_ i \in \mathbf{Z}$. The degree of $\mathcal{L}|_{C_ j}$ is $\sum _ j a_ j(C_ i \cdot C_ j)$. In particular $(\sum a_ i C_ i \cdot \sum a_ i C_ i) = 0$. Hence we see from Lemma <a href="/tag/0C66" data-tag="0C66">55.9.7</a> that $(a_1, \ldots , a_ n) = q(m_1, \ldots , m_ n)$ for some $q \in \mathbf{Q}$. Thus $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{O}_ X(lD)$ for some $l \in \mathbf{Z}$ where $D = \sum (m_ i/d) C_ i$ is as in Lemma <a href="/tag/0C67" data-tag="0C67">55.9.8</a> and we conclude. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>